"Modern skills are usually designed to rapidly reproduce a difficult but desirable spell."
"Skills are more flexible than spell impressions and reach complex results, but are harder to learn."
"Most modern skills top out between Levels 4 and 10; Level 10 means mastery."
"A maxed skill is complete, and its holder picks a new one."
"Leveling a skill once a year is common; twice is standard at Apex schools; many never level."
"A skill user's perception of the skill shapes what it can do."
"Summoners see Avowed abilities as data on their displays, such as a preservation talent."
"Skill names and descriptions on Earth are vague because a thousand committees shaped them."
"Skills can be read as spell code, and each species gets a redesigned version."
"Some skills top out at level 5, others at 10."
"A skill is never weaker, only its effects: it is more undeniable when used true to its nature."
"Rigid skills vary less; narrowed enough, a skill crosses into being a spell impression."
"Holders negotiate personalizations with the Contract, which must not conflict with the core."
"A narrow severing skill kills with less authority committed than a preservation skill."
"A skill is part of its holder; fatigue does not empty it like a tank, and it is always in some use."
"A holder may ask for a skill stripped of modernizations, closer to an older form."
"Some well-chosen skills show signs, like moths gathering, but many beautiful skills come without."
"Old skills designed for early Avowed are kept as historical artifacts in manuscript libraries."
"A holder can steer a skill beyond its designers' intent, as the Primary made fair play core to his."
"A first major skill stays prominent; a later major skill should run closer to it, not flee it."
